          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Rise to Fame</strong><br>\nThrough the 1840s and 1850s, Verdi produced many operas that remain in the repertory today, including \"Rigoletto,\" \"Il Trovatore,\" and \"La Traviata.\" His operas are known for their deep emotional content and complex characters.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Influence</strong><br>\nVerdi's music played a significant role in the Italian Risorgimento, with his works becoming symbols of the nationalistic movement seeking to unify Italy. His name itself was an acronym for \"Vittorio Emanuele Re D'Italia\" (Victor Emmanuel King of Italy).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Works</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Verdi composed some of his most profound operas, such as \"Aida,\" commissioned to celebrate the opening of the Suez Canal, and \"Otello\" and \"Falstaff,\" which were based on Shakespeare's plays.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
